Machine Learning IoT for Autism Spectrum Disorder:

This project is called Machine Learning IoT for Autism Spectrum Disorder, a healthcare-focused solution that combines IoT devices and Machine Learning for real-time behavioral monitoring and analysis of children with Autism Spectrum Disorder, commonly known as ASD.

The main objective of this project was to use physiological signals to help monitor and analyze behavioral patterns in children with ASD in a more efficient and data-driven manner.

The system is built using an ESP32 microcontroller, which acts as the central IoT device. It is connected to sensors such as a Heart Rate Sensor and a Galvanic Skin Response (GSR) Sensor.

The heart rate sensor measures the user's heartbeat, while the GSR sensor measures changes in skin conductivity, which can indicate stress, excitement, or emotional states.

The sensor data is continuously collected and transmitted for processing. The collected data is then analyzed using Python-based Machine Learning techniques.

For the machine learning component, I implemented a Random Forest algorithm, which was trained to identify patterns and classify behavioral conditions based on the sensor readings.

The system performs real-time analysis of physiological data and helps detect variations in behavior that may be useful for monitoring and support purposes.

